<script module>
  import Demo from "$ui/components/Demo.abide"
  import OnlineDemo from "$ui/demos/platform/observability/OnlineDemo.abide"
  import BundledDemo from "$ui/demos/platform/observability/BundledDemo.abide"
  import ObserveDemo from "$ui/demos/platform/observability/ObserveDemo.abide"
  import HealthFetchDemo from "$ui/demos/platform/observability/HealthFetchDemo.abide"
  import LogDemo from "$ui/demos/platform/observability/LogDemo.abide"
  import TraceClientDemo from "$ui/demos/platform/observability/TraceClientDemo.abide"
</script>


<main class="page">
  <h1>Observability</h1>
  <p class="lead">Four questions an app asks about itself, in increasing depth: <em>where am I running</em>, <em>is the server healthy</em>, <em>what happened during this request</em>, and <em>what should I record</em>. Each has one import and no plumbing.</p>
  <Demo id="online" title="online() — reactive connectivity probe" client="src/ui/demos/platform/observability/OnlineDemo.abide" tab="client">
    <p><code>online()</code> is a <em>reactive</em> connectivity boolean (true on the server; tracks <code>navigator.onLine</code> on the client, so this line re-renders when you go offline). Imported straight from <code>abide/shared/online</code> and called in the template — no RPC, no accessor plumbing.</p>
    <OnlineDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p>The cheapest probes: two booleans, imported and called straight in a template. <code>online()</code> is <em>reactive</em> — it tracks <code>navigator.onLine</code>, so the line here re-renders when you go offline (and is simply true on the server).</p>
  </aside>
  <Demo id="bundled" title="bundled() — desktop-bundle probe" client="src/ui/demos/platform/observability/BundledDemo.abide" tab="client">
    <p><code>bundled()</code> is true only inside the desktop bundle, false in the browser. Imported straight from <code>abide/ui/bundled</code> and called in the template.</p>
    <BundledDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p><code>bundled()</code> is true only inside the desktop bundle, false in a browser — the other one-import probe, called straight in the template.</p>
  </aside>
  <Demo id="health" title="health() — the merged health document" server="src/server/rpc/platformHealth.ts" client="src/ui/demos/platform/observability/HealthFetchDemo.abide" tab="client">
    <HealthFetchDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p>Same shape, one step out: <code>health()</code> is isomorphic, and the document is the same on both sides — composed in-process on the server, fetched from <code>/__abide/health</code> in the browser. App fields from <code>onHealth()</code> merge over the baseline either way, and their TYPES come from a generated <code>src/.abide/health.d.ts</code>, so <code>(await health()).bootId</code> is a typed read in this app and a compile error in one with no such hook. The second line proves it rather than asserting it: the server tab is an RPC that calls the same <code>health()</code> and reads <code>doc.bootId</code> straight off the document, where the checker is real. The browser half above cannot — it holds the document in a cell that widens to <code>any</code>, so nothing there consults the generated companion.</p>
  </aside>
  <Demo id="observe" title="trace() (via RPC)" server="src/server/rpc/platformObserve.ts" client="src/ui/demos/platform/observability/ObserveDemo.abide" tab="server">
    <ObserveDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p><code>trace()</code> returns the request's W3C traceparent — the id that ties this request to whatever called it, and to whatever it calls next. The router echoes it back on the response, so a trace crosses the boundary without you threading anything.</p>
  </aside>
  <Demo id="trace-client" title="trace() in the browser (adopted, never minted)" client="src/ui/demos/platform/observability/TraceClientDemo.abide" tab="client">
    <TraceClientDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p>Every request gets a traceparent whether or not a handler asks: the router propagates a well-formed incoming one and otherwise mints one, so every response carries <code>traceparent</code> + <code>traceresponse</code>. (The one exception is a content-addressed <code>/__abide/chunk/</code> asset — immutable, identity-free, joined by no span.)</p>
    <p><code>trace()</code> is isomorphic, so it answers in the browser too — with the id of the request that rendered the live page, re-adopted on every navigation. The client never mints one: an id generated in a tab would name a trace no server span belongs to.</p>
    <p>A browser RPC call carries it as a CHILD span — same trace id, fresh span id, which is what W3C asks of a caller — so the handler's work joins the page's trace. Click Run again above and the <code>traceId</code> holds while the span half moves. A navigation deliberately carries nothing: a nav is a new operation, and propagating would grow one immortal trace per tab.</p>
  </aside>
  <Demo id="log" title="default channel + <code>log.channel(name)</code> gating" server="src/server/rpc/platformLog.ts" client="src/ui/demos/platform/observability/LogDemo.abide" tab="server">
    <p><code>log</code> is isomorphic structured logging. Every line carries a <strong>channel</strong> label. The bare <code>log()</code> / <code>.info</code> / <code>.warn</code> / <code>.error</code> / <code>.trace</code> uses the <strong>default channel</strong> — the app name (<code>docs</code> here, from <code>package.json</code>) — and is always on. <code>log.channel(name)</code> names a channel gated by <code>DEBUG</code> (debug-npm style; the browser reads <code>localStorage.debug</code>), so framework internals under <code>abide:*</code> and verbose app channels stay quiet until asked for. A bare name is <strong>namespaced under the app</strong> for you — <code>channel('cards')</code> labels and gates as <code>docs:cards</code>, and <code>DEBUG=docs:*</code> lights every channel this app owns. <code>error</code> always emits, even on a gated channel. This RPC logs server-side (browser never sees the lines) and returns the fact that it logged:</p>
    <LogDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p><code>log()</code> writes on the server, so the card reports what the handler logged rather than showing it: an <code>info</code> line on the always-on default channel (the app name) and a <code>trace</code> line on the <code>DEBUG</code>-gated <code>docs:cards</code> channel. The gated one only reaches your terminal under <code>DEBUG=docs:cards</code>.</p>
  </aside>
</main>
